---
// Hand-written, not a content-collection entry: the recipes are TypeScript
// data (src/data/recipes.ts), not markdown, so this page builds its own
// heading list for DocsLayout's table of contents instead of getting one from
// remark. Each recipe's `id` doubles as its h2 anchor.
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { RECIPES } from '../../data/recipes';
import { inlineCode } from '../../inline-code';

const headings = RECIPES.map((recipe) => ({ depth: 2, slug: recipe.id, text: recipe.title }));
---

<DocsLayout title="Recipes" headings={headings} current="recipes">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      Four complete <code>accounts.toml</code> configurations, paste-ready. Each is checked at
      build time against the same schema <a href="/docs/config/">the annotated template</a> uses,
      so a recipe here either parses and matches the tool's own example, or the site does not
      ship.
    </p>
    <p>
      Copy the config into <code>~/.config/gitwho/accounts.toml</code>, replace the placeholder
      usernames, emails and hosts with your own, then run the <code>secret set</code> commands
      for whichever tokens it names. Then run <code>gitwho init --write</code> again — an account
      you have just added has no <code>includeIf</code> rule until you regenerate, so until you do,
      its repositories keep resolving to your global identity. Finish with
      <code>gitwho doctor</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    RECIPES.map((recipe) => (
      <section class="prose recipe">
        <h2 id={recipe.id}>{recipe.title}</h2>
        {/*
          `problem` is written with markdown backticks like the rest of the
          site's prose, but it is data rendered through an expression, not
          markdown, so nothing was turning them into anything and they reached
          the page as literal characters. Segments, not a string of HTML: each
          prose run stays a text node that Astro escapes, so the quotation marks
          in `includeIf "gitdir:"` never go near `set:html`.
        */}
        <p>
          {inlineCode(recipe.problem).map((segment) =>
            segment.code ? <code>{segment.text}</code> : segment.text,
          )}
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={recipe.toml} lang="toml" label={`the ${recipe.title} accounts.toml`} />
        {recipe.secrets.length > 0 && (
          <>
            <p class="label">store the tokens, then regenerate</p>
            {/*
              `init --write` is appended here rather than stored in the recipe
              data: `secrets` is exactly the `secret set` commands, and
              tests/recipes.test.ts cross-checks that list against the variables
              each account declares.
            */}
            <CodeBlock
              code={[...recipe.secrets, 'gitwho init --write'].join('\n')}
              lang="sh"
              label={`${recipe.title} setup commands`}
            />
          </>
        )}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Whichever recipe you started from, the last two steps are the same, in this order.
      <code>gitwho init --write</code> regenerates the identity rules from the config: it is
      idempotent, and it is what gives each account its <code>includeIf</code> entry. Skip it after
      adding an account and that account has no rule at all, so its repositories fall through to
      your global identity — quietly, and with the wrong name on every commit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then <code>gitwho doctor</code>. It is read-only, exits non-zero on problems, and never prints
      a secret value — so its output is always safe to paste when asking for help. It reports that
      the store's permissions are intact, that every token the config names is present, and that
      git's credential helper actually reaches gitwho. It does not compare the generated rules
      against your config, so it will not tell you that you forgot to regenerate.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .recipe {
    margin-top: var(--s7);
  }
</style>
